<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Yarkent Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1514 CE – 1705 CE (approx.) · capital Yarkent</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chagatai khanate of the western Tarim Basin, ruled from Yarkand, whose khans patronised poetry and Sufi orders until the Dzungars conquered it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1514 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-said-khan">Sultan Said Khan</a>, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-shule">Kashgar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlat</a> amir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-abu-bakr-dughlat">Mirza Aba Bakr</a>, founding a khanate with Yarkand as its capital. His cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-muhammad-haidar-dughlat">Mirza Muhammad Haidar Dughlat</a>, author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-i-rashidi">Tarikh-i Rashidi</a>, served at his court.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans ruled the oasis towns of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-shule">Kashgar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-khotan">Khotan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a>, with an agricultural economy and caravan trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rashid-khan">Abd al-Rashid Khan</a> the court was a centre of music, and his queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amannisa-khan">Amannisa Khan</a> is credited with compiling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghur-muqam">Uyghur Muqam</a>. In the 17th century the Naqshbandi Khoja families, divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqtaghliq">Aqtaghliq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarataghliq">Qarataghliq</a> factions, became increasingly powerful.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1680 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqtaghliq">Aqtaghliq</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afaq-khoja">Afaq Khoja</a> invited the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-boshugtu-khan">Galdan Boshugtu</a>, which conquered the khanate. The Khojas ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> until the Chagatai line was formally ended around 1705.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yarkent-khanate.jpg" alt="Yarkent Khanate" width="320" height="184"><figcaption>Image: naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Yarkent_Khanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1514 CE – 1705 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yarkent</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sultan Said Khan, Abd al-Rashid Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chagatai Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yarkand Khanate, Saidiya, Mamlakati Moghuliyya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1610&amp;focus=yarkent-khanate" aria-label="Yarkent Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6601 798 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yarkent Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6921 1054L6937 875M6921 1054L6928 868M6921 1054L7154 908M6921 1054L6951 879"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6937 875h0M6928 868h0M7154 908h0M6951 879h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="